1554107666015625, 0.01171875, 0.025604248046875, -0.0031070709228515625, -0.01490020751953125, -0.00238037109375, -0.00814056396484375, -0.00030994415283203125, -0.007904052734375, 0.0209197998046875, 0.0129852294921875, 0.00433349609375, -0.0262908935546875, 0.00621795654296875, 0.019775390625, 0.024627685546875, -0.0019426345825195312, -0.0156707763671875, 0.0109405517578125, -0.0195465087890625, 0.020751953125, -0.0201568603515625, 0.00872039794921875, -0.0084381103515625, -0.042327880859375, 0.00827789306640625, -0.01342010498046875, -0.008026123046875, -0.00574493408203125, 0.038818359375, 0.0168609619140625, 0.016265869140625, -0.0229644775390625, 0.00807952880859375, 0.0058746337890625, 0.0032215118408203125, 0.00988006591796875, 0.0013942718505859375, -0.023956298828125, 0.003765106201171875, -0.0277557373046875, -0.003414154052734375, 0.0008025169372558594, -0.026580810546875, 0.0306549072265625, -0.03363037109375, 0.004730224609375, -0.0218505859375, -0.020263671875, 0.0150299072265625, -0.0184478759765625, -0.0178375244140625, -0.005767822265625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\n# Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. In these systems, a *textual memory bank* refers to a structured collection of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback that the agent accumulates and references to inform future decisions. For example, an agent might store step-by-step solutions to previously solved tasks and consult them when facing similar challenges. A *self-improving loop* is the iterative process where the agent uses its memory to refine its strategies and performance over successive tasks, akin to a student reviewing past mistakes to improve future test scores. However, the reliability aspects of these methods\u2014specifically, their tendency to exhibit *fragility* (i.e., sensitivity to small changes in environment, task order, or feedback, leading to inconsistent or degraded performance)\u2014have been critically overlooked.\n\n# Methodology\n\nIn this work, we conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two representative memory-based methods. We broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple independent runs to quantify performance variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task sequencing. For each experimental condition, we measure agent performance on complex, multi-step tasks in simulated environments. The tasks include object assembly, navigation with obstacles, and multi-stage puzzle solving, each requiring the agent to recall and adapt previously learned strategies. The simulated environments are designed to introduce variability in task requirements and feedback, such as changing object properties or introducing ambiguous instructions.\n\nPerformance is quantified using task completion rate (percentage of tasks successfully completed), average reward per episode, and standard deviation across runs to capture variability. Statistical significance is assessed using paired t-tests and confidence intervals. For example, we report that in the default task order, agents achieve a mean completion rate of 78% (\u00b15%), while in randomized order, this drops to 62% (\u00b112%), with p < 0.01 indicating statistical significance.\n\nTo further probe the causes of observed fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory contents and introduce interventions aimed at reducing underspecification. Specifically, we augment the memory construction process with *detailed rubrics*\u2014explicit criteria for task success, such as checklists outlining required steps\u2014and *environment feedback*\u2014additional signals from the environment indicating partial progress or errors, such as real-time messages when the agent deviates from the optimal path. These enhancements are implemented by providing the agent with structured checklists and real-time feedback messages during task execution. For example, if a task requires assembling an object, the rubric specifies each assembly step, and the environment provides feedback if a step is skipped or performed incorrectly.\n\nThese methodological choices set the stage for our results, which reveal the specific ways in which memory-based agents are vulnerable to performance degradation. In the following section, we present quantitative and qualitative findings that illustrate these vulnerabilities.\n\n# Results\n\nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks; introducing a self-improving loop can further amplify this noise, as errors or suboptimal strategies may be reinforced over time. For instance, if an agent learns an incorrect solution early on\u2014such as skipping a crucial assembly step in a multi-part task\u2014it may repeatedly reference and propagate this error, leading to compounding failures. In one case study, an agent that mislearned the order of assembly steps failed 80% of subsequent related tasks, compared to a 25% failure rate when the correct sequence was learned.\n\nSecond, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, which acts as a hidden prerequisite for success. When task order is randomized, performance frequently degrades, revealing a lack of robustness. For example, agents exposed to more difficult tasks before mastering simpler ones often fail to generalize or adapt, resulting in lower completion rates and higher variance. In our experiments, the standard deviation of completion rates across runs increased from 5% in the default order to 12% in the randomized order, highlighting increased inconsistency.\n\n# Discussion\n\nManual inspection of agent memory suggests that task and environment underspecification\u2014where instructions or feedback lack sufficient detail\u2014contributes to this fragility. Specific persistent issues include: agents misinterpreting ambiguous instructions (e.g., unclear task goals such as \"assemble the object\" without specifying the order), failing to recover from incomplete or misleading feedback (e.g., missing error messages when a step is skipped), and struggling with unexpected environment dynamics (e.g., changes in task requirements not reflected in memory). For example, in one scenario, an agent failed to adapt when a familiar task introduced a new constraint, as the memory bank lacked relevant examples or clarifications.\n\nTo validate this hypothesis, we compare agent performance with and without the inclusion of detailed rubrics and environment feedback. Success is measured by the agent's ability to generalize improvements across varied task orders and by reductions in performance variance. With these interventions, agents show improved consistency and higher completion rates (e.g., completion rates increased from 62% to 71% in randomized order), and the standard deviation across runs decreased from 12% to 7%. However, persistent issues remain: agents still struggle with ambiguous instructions, incomplete feedback, and unexpected environment dynamics. For example, even with rubrics, agents sometimes misapply steps when faced with novel task variations, and environment feedback may not cover all possible errors. These *gaps* highlight the need for further research into more comprehensive specification and robust learning mechanisms. Alternative approaches, such as incorporating meta-learning strategies or adaptive feedback systems, may offer additional resilience but require further investigation.\n\n# Conclusion\n\nLooking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents. Specifically, we recommend: (1) reporting results across multiple independent runs to capture variance, (2) evaluating agents under randomized and adversarial task orderings, and (3) explicitly documenting any curricula or ordering assumptions. To implement these recommendations, researchers can adopt standardized reporting templates that include variance metrics, design experiments with shuffled and adversarial task sequences, and provide detailed documentation of task orderings and dependencies.\n\nAdditionally, our findings on underspecification call for the development of systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight. Concrete examples include interactive dashboards for monitoring agent memory (similar to model interpretability tools in machine learning, e.g., TensorBoard), tools for injecting clarifying feedback (such as annotation interfaces used in human-in-the-loop AI), and mechanisms for humans to audit or correct agent reasoning in real time (as seen in collaborative robotics platforms). For instance, a dashboard could allow users to flag ambiguous memory entries or inject clarifying instructions during task execution. These features would help prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways and support safer deployment in real-world settings.\n\"\"\""}}